{
  "requirement": "The SDK offers this `Sources` method and exposes the `search_structure`\ncommand through its source tools.\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Sources {\n    // ...\n\n    /// Search source structure.\n    pub async fn search_structure(\n        &self,\n        query: &StructuralQuery,\n    ) -> Result<StructuralMatches, SourceError>;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has two structural obligations. The first is a `Sources` method with the signature `pub async fn search_structure(&self, query: &StructuralQuery) -> Result<StructuralMatches, SourceError>`. The second is exposing the `search_structure` command through the SDK's source tools.\n\n**Method signature.** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#impl[Sources]/fn[search_structure]` matches the required signature exactly. It is `pub async`, takes `&self` and `query: &StructuralQuery`, and returns `Result<StructuralMatches, SourceError>`. It delegates to `self.analysis.search_structure(query)`. That is `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources.rs#impl[SourceAnalysis<P>]/fn[search_structure]`, which calls `self.provider.search_structure(query, &self.languages)`. The provider trait method, `SourceProvider#fn[search_structure]` in `sources.rs`, has a default that goes to `defaults::search_structure`. That default returns `Result<StructuralMatches, P::Error>`, and the `Sources` wrapper uses `SourceError`.\n\n**Tool exposure.** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/sources.rs#impl[SourceSession<…>/ToolSession<D>]/fn[definitions]` declares a `search_structure` tool. Its required parameters are `language` and `rule`, and its optional parameters are `prefix` and `limit`. In the same impl, `fn[dispatch]` handles the `\"search_structure\"` name. It reads `language`, `rule`, the scope and the limit from the arguments, and builds a `StructuralQuery`. It then calls `self.tools.analysis.search_structure(&StructuralQuery{…})`, which is the `SourceAnalysis` method above. The project-sources session in `agent/project_sources.rs` (`fn[definitions]` and `fn[dispatch]`) also exposes the tool and calls `sources.search_structure` with a `StructuralQuery`. So the command is exposed through the tools and routed to the SDK method.\n\n**Reference markings.** Each root carries an `{impl}` reference to the requirement, and the helper path is reached from those roots. I found no conflicting or missing structural element. The runtime behaviors of the tool (`search_structure.behavior` and `search_structure.inputs`) belong to separate requirements and are outside this one's structural scope. No `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.",
